# Binary long division. Answers quotient, then r, then remainder.
#
#   rw progs/bindiv.rw '<11)1011>'   ->   11r10        (1011 / 11 = 11 rem 10)
#
# The divisor comes first, because the tape is laid out the way the sum is
# written by hand: 11 goes into 1011. Putting it there also puts the dividend
# at the far right, next to the remainder, which is what makes taking the next
# bit a single rule instead of a token walking the length of the tape.
#
#   < divisor ^ ; quotient | remainder = $ dividend >
#
# Per dividend bit: shift it into the remainder, then decide. Deciding is what
# makes this harder than binmul.rw -- everything up to now could ripple, but
# this has to know whether the divisor fits before it commits.
#
# So there are two passes over the divisor and one mechanism. @ walks it left
# emitting one traveller per bit, exactly as in binmul.rw, and what the
# traveller does on arrival depends on the marker it lands against:
#
#   % &   compare: subtract without writing, just to see if it borrows out
#   : !   subtract for real
#
# A borrow out the left of the remainder means the divisor did not fit, so the
# quotient bit is 0 and the remainder is untouched -- which is why the compare
# has to leave the digits alone. Otherwise the same walk runs again against
# : and the digits are written, and the quotient bit is 1.

# bootstrap: ) is consumed, so this fires exactly once
)->^;|=$

# a traveller on its way right. First, so a traveller in flight freezes
# everything else, which is what keeps them one at a time.
T0->0T
T1->1T
T;->;T
T|->|T
U0->0U
U1->1U
U;->;U
U|->|U

# compare column: remainder digit, divisor bit, borrow in. The digit is
# written back unchanged; only the marker carries anything away.
0T%->%0
1T%->%1
0U%->&0
1U%->%1
0T&->&0
1T&->%1
0U&->&0
1U&->&1
|T%->|%
|U%->|&
|T&->|&
|U&->|&

# subtract column: same arithmetic, but now the digit is replaced
0T:->:0
1T:->:1
0U:->!1
1U:->:0
0T!->!1
1T!->:0
0U!->!0
1U!->!1
|T:->|:
|U:->|!
|T!->|!
|U!->|!

# @ walks the divisor leftwards, putting each bit back as it steps over it
0@->@0T
1@->@1U
<@-><H

# divisor spent: ripple the last borrow through the rest of the remainder
0&->&0
1&->%1
|&->|l
0!->!1
1!->:0
|!->|:

# the divisor pointer walks home. Above the marker walks and above anything
# that launches F, so F is never sent while ^ is still missing.
H0->0H
H1->1H
H;->^;

# F goes back to wake the divisor pointer for a pass
0F->F0
1F->F1
|F->F|
;F->F;
^F->@

# n and m carry the decided quotient bit back to the quotient
0n->n0
1n->n1
|n->0|
0m->m0
1m->m1
|m->1|

# it did not fit: park the marker idle and send a 0 to the quotient
l0->0l
l1->1l
l$->n=$

# it did fit and has been subtracted: park idle, send a 1
:0->0:
:1->1:
:$->m=$

# it did fit: park the marker in subtract mode and run the walk again
%0->0%
%1->1%
%$->F:$

# next dividend bit. Last, like every launch in progs/: the walks above all
# move by swapping, and a launch any higher would fire mid-transit.
=$0->F0%$
=$1->F1%$

# dividend spent: strip the divisor and the scaffolding
<0-><
<1-><
<^;->[
[00->[0
[01->[1
[->
|00->|0
|01->|1
|->r
=$>->
